<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Haiti</h1><p class="st-meta">1811 CE – 1820 CE · capital Cap-Henri</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Henri Christophe's northern Haitian monarchy, with a new nobility, the palace of Sans-Souci and the colossal Citadelle Laferrière.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-haiti">State of Haiti</a> in the north became a kingdom in March 1811, when a council of state proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henri-christophe">Henri Christophe</a> king. He was crowned Henri I at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cap-haitien">Cap-Haïtien</a>, renamed Cap-Henri, in June 1811, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandre-petion">Alexandre Pétion's</a> <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-haiti-1806-1849">Republic of Haiti</a> continued to rule the south and west.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Henri created a hereditary nobility of dukes, counts and barons, a royal army and a legal code, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/code-henry">Code Henry</a>. Plantation labour was enforced to keep <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> exports high, making the kingdom relatively wealthy. He built the palace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sans-souci-palace">Sans-Souci</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milot">Milot</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/citadelle-laferriere">Citadelle Laferrière</a> on a mountaintop against a feared French return, and he invited English teachers to found schools. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> was the state religion.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In August 1820 Henri suffered a stroke; troops mutinied and he shot himself on 8 October 1820. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-pierre-boyer">Jean-Pierre Boyer</a> then reunited the north with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-haiti-1806-1849">Republic of Haiti</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/citadelle-laferriere">Citadelle</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sans-souci-palace">Sans-Souci</a> are now a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-haiti.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Haiti" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Saul ip Code cleanup by Mnmazur · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Haiti_(1964%E2%80%931986,_civil).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1811 CE – 1820 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Cap-Henri</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Henri I</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Haitian Creole and French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Haiti</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Hayti, Royaume d'Haïti</dd></dl></aside></div>
